[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question, then I'll write up the design. This one is about the actual stated goal — debugging [3mproduction[0m issues
  means being able to turn detail up on a live system without a redeploy.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether log level is fixed at ship time or adjustable at runtime, and if adjustable, by whom. A URL
  parameter in the browser is the difference between "ask the user to reproduce while we watch" and "wait for the next
  release" — but it's also a control anyone can flip, so verbose logs become reachable by any visitor.

  - [1mRuntime-configurable both sides:[0m Node reads [38;5;153mLOG_LEVEL[39m env var; browser reads [38;5;153mlocalStorage.logLevel[39m with a
    [38;5;153m?logLevel=debug[39m URL override. Buys live diagnosis on a real user's session; costs that debug output is publicly
    togglable — which is exactly why the redaction denylist has to be solid.
  - [1mEnv var only, browser fixed:[0m Node adjustable, browser level baked in at [38;5;153minfo[39m. Buys no public toggle; costs the
    browser half of production debugging, which is the half you asked about.
  - [1mFixed everywhere:[0m one constant in the file. Simplest; means changing log level is a code change and a deploy.

  I recommend runtime-configurable on both sides. Given we already decided the logger scrubs sensitive keys, the
  marginal risk of a visitor enabling debug is low, and without it the browser logger can't do the job it's being built
  for.
